steps.body:
A backtest scans every tick of a trading day, and most of its time goes to waiting for memory. *Memory Hierarchy: Registers, Caches, RAM, Pages, and Disk* showed why: the CPU moves whole cache lines (64 bytes on x86-64, 128 on Apple Silicon), a hit in L1 takes about a nanosecond and a miss to RAM about 100, and the prefetcher fetches ahead only when the next address is predictable. Its model cache counted the misses. Here is what that means for the containers that hold ticks:

- A `std::vector<Tick>` is one contiguous block, so a scan uses every byte of every line it loads and the prefetcher stays ahead.
- A `std::list<Tick>` puts each tick in its own 56-byte heap node, the tick plus two pointers. The next node's address is only known once the current one has arrived, so when the nodes are scattered every step is a trip to RAM.
- `std::vector<Tick>` is an **array of structs** (AoS): a loop that reads one field still drags all 40 bytes of each tick through the cache. A **struct of arrays** (SoA) keeps one vector per field, so a loop over bid sizes reads 8 bytes a tick.

```cpp
struct TickColumns {   // SoA: one column per field
    std::vector<int64_t> ts_ns;
    std::vector<double> bid, ask, bid_sz, ask_sz;
};
std::cout << sizeof(TickColumns) << '\n';  // 120, five 24-byte vector headers
```

Keep AoS where code uses whole ticks together, like a strategy reacting to one tick at a time. Use columns for scans over a few fields, like a backtest computing one feature over a whole day.

example.code:
// -O2
#include <cstdint>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <list>
#include <vector>

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;          // nanoseconds since midnight
    double bid, ask;        // prices
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;  // sizes
};

int main() {
    const int n = 1'000'000;
    std::vector<Tick> ticks;  // array of structs: whole ticks side by side
    ticks.reserve(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        double bid = 100.0 + (i * 7919LL % 400) * 0.25;  // prices jump around
        ticks.push_back({i, bid, bid + 0.25, 1.0 + i % 5, 1.0 + i % 3});
    }

    std::list<Tick> by_price(ticks.begin(), ticks.end());  // one heap node per tick
    by_price.sort([](const Tick& a, const Tick& b) { return a.bid < b.bid; });

    std::vector<double> bid_sz;  // struct of arrays: one column per field
    bid_sz.reserve(n);
    for (const Tick& t : ticks) {
        bid_sz.push_back(t.bid_sz);
    }

    double from_vector = 0, from_list = 0, from_column = 0;
    for (const Tick& t : ticks) from_vector += t.bid_sz;
    for (const Tick& t : by_price) from_list += t.bid_sz;
    for (double s : bid_sz) from_column += s;

    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(0);
    std::cout << "vector: " << from_vector << ", list: " << from_list << ", column: " << from_column << '\n';
    std::cout << "bytes per tick read: " << sizeof(Tick) << " vs " << sizeof(double) << '\n';
    std::cout << "cache lines read: " << n * sizeof(Tick) / 64 << " vs " << n * sizeof(double) / 64 << '\n';
    return 0;
}

