steps.body:
An exchange feed arrives as packets of raw bytes, each message's fields at fixed offsets with no padding between them. *Binary Formats: Bit-Fields, Unions, and Endianness* decoded one field by field with `std::memcpy` and byte swaps, and *Files: Streams, Buffers, and the Disk* wrote raw values to a file and mapped one with `mmap`. This part moves whole structs, so the layout itself has to match the wire.

`#pragma pack(push, 1)` tells the compiler (g++, clang, and MSVC all accept it) to lay out the structs that follow with no padding, and `#pragma pack(pop)` ends it:

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

#pragma pack(push, 1)
struct WireHeader {
    char type;
    uint32_t seq;  // offset 1, not 4
};
#pragma pack(pop)

int main() {
    std::cout << sizeof(WireHeader) << ' ' << alignof(WireHeader) << '\n';  // 5 1
}
```

Move bytes in and out with `std::memcpy`, and only for **trivially copyable** types, which `std::is_trivially_copyable_v` from *Compile-Time C++: constexpr, static_assert, and Type Traits* checks. A struct holding a `std::string` isn't one, since the string's bytes are a pointer to its text. A `std::array<unsigned char, N>` makes a good packet buffer: fixed size, no heap allocation, and it knows its length. `packet.data()` is a pointer to its first byte, and `.data()` does the same for a vector.

Never decode by casting the pointer, as in `*reinterpret_cast<const WireQuote*>(p)`. No `WireQuote` object lives at those bytes, so reading through it is undefined behavior, the `reinterpret_cast` rule from *Conversions and Casts* and the strict aliasing rule from *Binary Formats*. The copy is free anyway: at `-O2`, `memcpy` of an `int64_t` from offset 5 is one load on both machines.

```text
x86-64, g++ 10.2 (the sandbox)     ARM64, Apple clang (a Mac)
mov rax, QWORD PTR [rdi+5]         ldur x0, [x0, #5]
```

Packed fields have a cost. g++ 10.2 lets you bind an `int64_t&` to one without a warning, yet the field is misaligned, which x86-64 and Apple Silicon tolerate and stricter CPUs don't. So pack only at the wire, and copy each message into a normal struct like `Tick` as soon as it arrives.

example.code:
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <type_traits>

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;          // nanoseconds since midnight
    double bid, ask;        // prices
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;  // sizes
};

#pragma pack(push, 1)
struct WireQuote {    // the feed's layout: no padding, prices with 4 implied decimals
    char type;        // 'Q'
    uint32_t seq;
    int64_t ts_ns;
    int32_t bid_px, ask_px;
    uint32_t bid_sz, ask_sz;
};
#pragma pack(pop)

struct NamedTick {
    std::string symbol;
    Tick tick;
};

static_assert(std::is_trivially_copyable_v<WireQuote>, "memcpy needs a trivially copyable type");

int main() {
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << "WireQuote: size " << sizeof(WireQuote) << ", align " << alignof(WireQuote)
              << ", ts_ns at " << offsetof(WireQuote, ts_ns) << '\n';
    std::cout << "trivially copyable: Tick " << std::is_trivially_copyable_v<Tick>
              << ", NamedTick " << std::is_trivially_copyable_v<NamedTick> << '\n';

    std::array<unsigned char, 64> packet{};  // the bytes as they arrive
    const WireQuote sent[2] = {{'Q', 1, 34'200'000'000'000, 1012500, 1012600, 300, 200},
                               {'Q', 2, 34'200'000'250'000, 1012600, 1012700, 100, 400}};
    std::size_t used = 0;
    for (const WireQuote& q : sent) {
        std::memcpy(packet.data() + used, &q, sizeof q);
        used += sizeof q;
    }
    std::cout << "packet bytes used: " << used << '\n';

    for (std::size_t at = 0; at + sizeof(WireQuote) <= used; at += sizeof(WireQuote)) {
        WireQuote q;
        std::memcpy(&q, packet.data() + at, sizeof q);  // copy out, never cast the pointer
        Tick t{q.ts_ns, q.bid_px / 10000.0, q.ask_px / 10000.0, double(q.bid_sz), double(q.ask_sz)};
        std::cout << "seq " << q.seq << ": " << t.bid_sz << " @ " << t.bid << " / " << t.ask << " @ " << t.ask_sz << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

