steps.body:
A risk system often gives each thread a counter of its own: orders sent, fills seen, one strategy's position. No locks are needed, since no two threads write the same variable. But when two of those counters share a cache line, the threads collide anyway. A core must own a line exclusively to write it, so each write by one core takes the line away from the others, and it bounces between them. That's **false sharing**: no data is shared, only the line.

Four threads each adding 1 to its own counter 5 × 10^6 times took roughly 70 to 150 ms here with the counters 8 bytes apart, 18 to 47 ms with them 64 bytes apart, and 10 to 25 ms 128 bytes apart, where one thread alone took about 9 ms, rough times from this emulated sandbox. The totals were right every time: false sharing costs only time, which is why it hides so well. *Atomics and Memory Ordering*, in the Concurrency section, runs this test with real threads.

The fix is to give each hot counter a line of its own with `alignas`:

```cpp
struct alignas(128) Slot {
    long count;
};
std::cout << sizeof(Slot) << ' ' << alignof(Slot) << '\n';  // 128 128
```

`alignas(N)` raises the alignment of a type or a member to `N`, and `sizeof` rounds up with it, so an array of `Slot` puts each one on a line of its own. Which `N`? x86-64 servers use 64-byte lines, but Intel CPUs often fetch them in adjacent pairs. Apple Silicon uses 128, and since this sandbox's x86-64 runs emulated on an Apple Silicon Mac, 64 wasn't always enough above. So 128 is the safer pad, and the one that suits both. C++17 names the value `std::hardware_destructive_interference_size`, in `<new>`, but it's the compiler's guess for its target, as *Where a Feature Comes From: Standard, Compiler, OS, Hardware* showed, and g++ 10.2 has none, so code usually names its own constant. Padding costs memory, so pad only the few variables that different threads write constantly.

example.code:
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

// x86-64 servers use 64-byte cache lines, Apple Silicon 128. Padding to 128 suits both.
constexpr std::size_t kPad = 128;

struct RiskCounters {  // the order thread bumps orders, the fill thread bumps fills
    long orders;
    long fills;
};

struct PaddedRiskCounters {
    alignas(kPad) long orders;
    alignas(kPad) long fills;
};

struct alignas(kPad) StrategySlot {  // one per strategy thread
    long position;
};

int main() {
    std::cout << "RiskCounters: size " << sizeof(RiskCounters) << ", fills "
              << offsetof(RiskCounters, fills) - offsetof(RiskCounters, orders) << " bytes after orders\n";
    std::cout << "PaddedRiskCounters: size " << sizeof(PaddedRiskCounters) << ", align "
              << alignof(PaddedRiskCounters) << ", fills "
              << offsetof(PaddedRiskCounters, fills) - offsetof(PaddedRiskCounters, orders) << " bytes after orders\n";
    std::array<StrategySlot, 4> slots{};
    std::cout << "4 slots: " << sizeof(slots) << " bytes, " << sizeof(StrategySlot) << " bytes apart\n";
    return 0;
}

